  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 357 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nThis early morning, 06Z RAP analysis shows a wedge of 1012 mb \nsurface high pressure extending across the UP, while in Canada GOES \nwater vapor imagery shows that tonight's Quebec low resides just \neast of James Bay. Local observations show that temperatures have \ndecreased into the 50s (60s if in proximity to a Great Lake), and a \nlook out the window indicates a clear sky with visible stars and a \nwaning gibbous moon.\n\nThese clear-sky conditions are expected to change throughout the day \ntoday as a pair of shortwaves ride through the region tonight into \ntomorrow morning. Embedded within WNW'ly gradient flow and grazing \nthe WI border Thursday afternoon, measurable precipitation from this \nfirst wave is anticipated in the southern reaches of the UP near \nMarinette, which may receive up to 0.10\" and a thunderstorm or two. \nThe second shortwave arrives in the overnight hours of early Friday \nmorning, traversing the western UP within NW'ly flow. While most of \nthe action from this impulse will manifest south of the UP, CAMs at \n24 hours out indicate considerable spread in the exact placement of \nthunderstorm development. While PoPs range from 20-50% across the UP \nwith up to 0.20\" (best and highest chances south and west), a strong \nthunderstorm can't be ruled out along the WI border or down in \nMenominee County. Notable with this system is strongly veering wind \nprofile with elevated CAPE; low-end easterly surface winds give way \nto ~40 kts of WNW'ly flow at 6 km, and some soundings indicate \nelevated CAPE upwards of 500 J/kg for locations along the WI border.\n\nAs the Quebec low progresses eastward, it will be replaced by \nsignificant upper-level ridging over the central CONUS, resulting in \nnorthwest flow across the Great Lakes for the weekend. High \ntemperatures are expected to remain above-normal in the mid to upper \n70s at least through Sunday due to the influence of this ridging \npattern. Early next week brings the next best chance for \nprecipitation, with current model guidance indicating a 40-60% \nchance of at least 0.25 inches of rain by 8am Wednesday, however, \nuncertainty is still high at this time for the timing and extent of \nprecipitation. Breezy conditions are anticipated as this system \nmoves through. NBM probabilities of wind gusts at or above 40mph are \nbetween 30-60% in the Keweenaw, highest on Wednesday in the wake of \nthe cold front.",
